export anchor DuplicateExports:
    description:
        A module exports each name once. There are four ways a name can leave
        a module. It can be a declaration marked export, `export Name`,
        `from ./X export Name`, or `from ./X export *`, which hands on
        everything X exports.
        When two of them export the same name and it means two different
        things, that is a compile error, `duplicate-export`. It doesn't matter
        whether the exports are written out or come in through `export *`.

        ```piton fragment
        \\\
        from ./cruxible export *
        from ./strataglyph export *
        \\\
        ```

        If both modules export an anchor called Roles, nobody importing Roles
        from this index can tell which one they would get, so the second line
        is an error. The error names the clashing name and both modules it
        comes from. To fix it, export one of them under an alias, or replace
        `export *` with an explicit list that leaves one out or renames it.

        ```piton fragment
        \\\
        from ./cruxible export *
        from ./strataglyph export Roles StrataglyphRoles, Glyphs
        \\\
        ```

        Exporting the same thing twice under one name isn't a collision. Two
        `export *` lines that both end up at the same anchor (say, because both
        modules re-export it from a shared file) export one thing, and that's
        fine.

        A duplicate export is still an error, but the compiler keeps one of
        the two so the rest of the program can resolve and every other problem
        gets reported in the same run. A declaration marked export wins
        first, then the other named exports in the order they are written,
        then the `export *` lines in the order they are written. The one that
        isn't kept isn't exported by the module, so if nothing else reaches it,
        `piton reach` reports it as unreachable. The language server reports
        the same error, since it shares the compiler's diagnostics.
